{"data":{"attachmentkeynames":["rs"],"desc":"\"This is working now\" — merge the current feature branch into the repo's default branch (local master, else main) and push it upstream, return to the feature branch, then bring the branch up to date with the merge commit it just made. Transactional: requires that you are ON the branch being merged, no conflicts, no merge in flight; checkout default → merge --no-ff → push origin <default> → checkout back → dev.git.update_from_master(repo, branch), bailing with the step's stderr on any failure up to the push. The closing update exists because --no-ff always creates a commit on the default branch that the feature branch lacks: update_from_master fetches, merges it in (normally a fast-forward), runs the install step (dev.git.after_update - a no-op unless master carried other work) and pushes the branch when it tracks a remote. A failed update does NOT fail the command - the merge is already pushed; it is reported in msg and under `update`. A dirty tree is NOT a blocker: git itself refuses a checkout or merge that would overwrite an edit (gitrun reports the refusal verbatim and the command steps back to the branch with nothing changed), and edits it does not touch ride along untouched, reported as `carried` (Cargo.lock, rewritten by every build, is the standing case). A real merge conflict is reported with the tree left mid-merge on the default branch (resolve then commit, or dev.git merge --abort) — never silently half-merged. Returns {status, msg, branch, default, carried, restart (from the update's install step), update (update_from_master's result), steps (the update's steps prefixed \"update: \")}.","import":"use ndata::dataarray::DataArray;","params":[{"name":"repo","type":"String"},{"name":"branch","type":"String"}],"returntype":"FLAT","type":"rust"},"id":"rwuprt1a05cfd1d26o118","readers":[],"time":1795197971149,"username":"system","writers":[]}